{"timestamp": "2025-09-12T19:57:26.252138", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # Check if the item is in the cookbook\n  if item not in env.world.cookbook.index:\n      return []\n\n  # Get the item index\n  item_index = env.world.cookbook.index[item]\n\n  # Check if the item is a primitive (no need to craft)\n  if item_index in env.world.cookbook.primitives:\n      return []\n\n  # Get the recipe for the item\n  if item_index not in env.world.cookbook.recipes:\n      # This should not happen, but return empty list if it does\n      return []\n\n  recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes[item_index]\n\n  # Check all ingredients (skip \"_at\")\n  for ingredient_index, amount in recipe.items():\n      if ingredient_index == \"_at\":\n          continue\n      # If the ingredient is not in the inventory or not enough, return empty list\n      if ingredient_index >= len(env._current_state.inventory) or env._current_state.inventory[ingredient_index] < amount:\n          return []\n\n  # Get the workshop location from the recipe\n  workshop_location = recipe.get(\"_at\", None)\n  if not workshop_location:\n      return []\n\n  # Extract the workshop number (e.g., \"workshop0\" -> 0)\n  try:\n      workshop_num = int(workshop_location[-1])\n  except:\n      return []\n\n  # Check if workshop_num is valid (should be 0, 1, or 2)\n  if workshop_num < 0 or workshop_num >= len(env.world.workshop_indices):\n      return []\n\n  # Get the workshop kind index\n  workshop_index = env.world.workshop_indices[workshop_num]\n\n  # Check if the agent is currently at a workshop\n  # If not, we need to move to the workshop\n  # We'll check if the agent is next to the workshop\n  if not env._current_state.next_to(workshop_index):\n      # We need to move to the workshop\n      # We return a move action to the workshop.\n      # But how do we know which direction to move? This is a simplification.\n      # For now, we assume that the agent can move to the workshop by going in a fixed direction\n      # However, this is a problem because we don't know the direction.\n      # We'll return a move action to the right for now (as a placeholder)\n      return [3]  # RIGHT\n\n  # If we are next to the workshop, we can use it\n  return [4]  # USE", "island_id": 8, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
{"timestamp": "2025-09-12T19:57:28.236376", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # Check if the item is in the cookbook\n  if item not in env.world.cookbook.index:\n      return []\n\n  item_index = env.world.cookbook.index[item]\n\n  # Check if it's a primitive (no need to craft)\n  if item_index in env.world.cookbook.primitives:\n      return []\n\n  # Get the recipe\n  recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes.get(item_index)\n  if recipe is None:\n      return []\n\n  # Check ingredients\n  for ingredient_index, amount in recipe.items():\n      if ingredient_index == \"_at\":\n          continue\n      if ingredient_index >= len(env._current_state.inventory) or env._current_state.inventory[ingredient_index] < amount:\n          return []\n\n  # Get workshop location\n  workshop_location = recipe.get(\"_at\")\n  if workshop_location is None:\n      return []\n\n  # Extract workshop number from workshop_location (e.g., \"workshop0\" -> 0)\n  try:\n      workshop_num = int(workshop_location[-1])\n  except:\n      return []\n\n  # Get the workshop index from the environment's workshop_indices\n  if workshop_num < 0 or workshop_num >= len(env.world.workshop_indices):\n      return []\n\n  workshop_index = env.world.workshop_indices[workshop_num]\n\n  # We need to move to the workshop location (not just assume we are there)\n  # We will use the next_to method to check if there's a workshop in the 3x3 neighborhood.\n\n  # Check if we are next to the workshop\n  if env._current_state.next_to(workshop_index):\n      # We are next to the workshop, so we can just use it\n      return [4]\n\n  # If not, we need to move towards the workshop.\n  # We'll use a simple movement strategy: try to move in a direction that gets us closer to the workshop.\n  # But note: we don't have direct access to the workshop's location in the environment.\n\n  # Instead, we can rely on the environment's next_to method, but we don't know the workshop's location.\n  # We have to move until we are next to the workshop.\n\n  # However, the environment doesn't provide a way to find the workshop's location. So we'll have to assume that the agent can move freely until it is next to the workshop.\n\n  # We'll return a sequence of moves (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT) to get to the workshop.\n  # But note: we don't know the direction. We'll have to try all directions until we are next to the workshop.\n\n  # However, the problem is that we don't have a way to know the direction of the workshop.\n\n  # So we will return a sequence of moves that might get us closer to the workshop, but we don't know which direction.\n  # This is a limitation of the current environment.\n\n  # Since we don't have a way to find the workshop, we'll just return [4] if we are next to it, otherwise we return an empty list?\n  # But that would mean we cannot craft if we are not next to the workshop.\n\n  # We need to move until we are next to the workshop. But we don't have a pathfinding algorithm.\n\n  # Given the constraints, we'll assume that the agent is already at the workshop, so we return [4] if next_to is True, otherwise we return an empty list meaning we cannot craft.\n\n  # Therefore, we'll return [4] only if we are next to the workshop, otherwise an empty list.\n\n  # But note: the environment might have multiple workshops, but we are using the specific workshop_index.\n  # We are checking if we are next to the workshop (i.e., any cell in the 3x3 neighborhood has the workshop kind).\n\n  # So the code above returns [4] if next_to, otherwise [].\n\n  # However, we are not actually moving to the workshop. We are just checking if we are next to it.\n\n  # This is a problem: we are not generating the movement actions to get to the workshop.\n\n  # We cannot generate the movement actions without knowing the direction to the workshop.\n\n  # So for now, we'll assume that the agent is already at the workshop (next_to returns True) and return [4].\n\n  # If the agent is not at the workshop, we return [] meaning we cannot craft.\n\n  # This is a limitation of the current implementation.\n\n  return [4] if env._current_state.next_to(workshop_index) else []", "island_id": 8, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
{"timestamp": "2025-09-12T19:57:53.953647", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # Check if the item is craftable at all\n  if item not in env.world.cookbook.index:\n      return []\n\n  # Get the index of the item from the cookbook\n  item_idx = env.world.cookbook.index[item]\n\n  # Check if the item is a primitive item (if it's in the primitives set)\n  if item_idx in env.world.cookbook.primitives:\n      # Primitives can be collected directly, not crafted\n      return []\n\n  # Get the recipe for the item\n  recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes.get(item_idx, {})\n  if not recipe:\n      # If there's no recipe, the item is not craftable\n      return []\n\n  # Check if we have all the required ingredients\n  # We'll need to get the primitives required for each ingredient\n  # This is where the logic gets complex. We'll need to check the inventory for each ingredient\n\n  # For now, assume we have all the ingredients\n  # This is a placeholder for the actual inventory check logic\n\n  # Get the workshop location where the item must be crafted\n  workshop_idx = recipe.get('_at', None)\n  if workshop_idx is None:\n      # If no workshop is specified, assume it's craftable anywhere (not typical)\n      return []\n\n  # Convert workshop_idx to a string (it's probably a string like \"workshop0\")\n  workshop_name = env.world.cookbook.index.get(workshop_idx)\n  if workshop_name is None:\n      return []\n\n  # Now, we need to move to the workshop location\n  # First, find the position of the workshop in the grid\n  workshop_pos = None\n  for i in range(env.world.cookbook.world.shape[0]):\n      for j in range(env.world.cookbook.world.shape[1]):\n          if env.world.cookbook.world[i, j] == workshop_name:\n              workshop_pos = (i, j)\n              break\n      if workshop_pos:\n          break\n\n  if workshop_pos is None:\n      return []\n\n  # Move to the workshop position\n  actions = []\n  # Calculate the direction to move to reach the workshop\n  # This is a simplified movement logic\n  current_pos = env._current_state.pos\n  dx = workshop_pos[0] - current_pos[0]\n  dy = workshop_pos[1] - current_pos[1]\n\n  # Move horizontally first (left/right)\n  if dx > 0:\n      actions.append(1)  # DOWN\n  elif dx < 0:\n      actions.append(0)  # UP\n\n  # Move vertically (up/down)\n  if dy > 0:\n      actions.append(3)  # RIGHT\n  elif dy < 0:\n      actions.append(2)  # LEFT\n\n  # Now, perform the USE action at the workshop\n  actions.append(4)  # USE\n\n  return actions", "island_id": 0, "scores": {"3": -1}}
{"timestamp": "2025-09-12T19:57:55.961254", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # Check if the item is craftable (i.e., it has a recipe in the cookbook).\n  # If not, return empty list.\n  # Get the recipe from the cookbook for the item.\n  # Check if we have all the required primitives for crafting the item.\n  # If not, return empty list.\n  # If we have all the required primitives, then we need to go to the workshop and craft the item.\n  # The workshop location is specified in the recipe (e.g., workshop0, workshop1, workshop2).\n  # For now, let's assume the workshop is workshop0 (we need to handle all workshops).\n\n  # Step 1: Check if item is craftable.\n  # Step 2: Get recipe and workshop.\n  # Step 3: Check if we have required items (primitives).\n  # Step 4: Move to workshop (if not already at workshop).\n  # Step 5: Craft the item (USE action).\n\n  # Note: We have to handle the case where the workshop location is not at the current position.\n\n  return []", "island_id": 0, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
