{"timestamp": "2025-09-12T20:55:19.385609", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # We need to craft the item. Let's get the recipe for the item.\n  # This will return a dictionary of required items with their quantities.\n  # The recipe will have a key \"_at\" which specifies the workshop where it must be crafted.\n  recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes.get(item)\n  if recipe is None:\n    return []  # Item not craftable\n\n  # Get the workshop index where the recipe must be crafted.\n  workshop = recipe[\"_at\"]\n  workshop_index = env.world.cookbook.index[workshop]  # This is the index of the workshop in the grid\n\n  # Check if the agent has all the required items in inventory.\n  # We'll iterate over the recipe items.\n  # The recipe is a dictionary where keys are item names and values are quantities.\n  for ingredient, quantity in recipe.items():\n    if ingredient == \"_at\":\n      continue  # Skip the workshop key\n    ingredient_index = env.world.cookbook.index[ingredient]\n    if env._current_state.inventory[ingredient_index] < quantity:\n      # If the agent doesn't have enough of an ingredient, we need to collect it first.\n      # So we return an empty list for now, since we cannot craft without all ingredients.\n      return []\n\n  # If we have all the ingredients, we need to go to the workshop and craft.\n  # We'll move to the workshop location.\n  # First, find the nearest workshop location on the grid.\n  # The workshop is a specific kind of cell, and we need to move to it.\n  # We'll use the `next_to` method of the state to find if we are next to the workshop.\n  # But we need to actually move to the workshop. So we'll need to generate a path to the workshop.\n  # We'll use a BFS or similar to find the shortest path to the workshop.\n  # However, for simplicity, let's assume we have a way to move to the workshop.\n  # We'll generate a list of move actions to get to the workshop.\n  # We'll use the `move_to` function to get the sequence of moves.\n\n  # Since we don't have a `move_to` function, we'll implement it here.\n  # But note: We are not supposed to implement new functions, only to implement `craft_v1` as per the DSL.\n  # We'll assume that the environment has a way to move to the nearest workshop.\n\n  # Let's get the current position of the agent.\n  current_pos = env._current_state.pos\n  # We need to find a cell with the workshop kind.\n  workshop_pos = None\n  for i in range(env.world.world_size):\n    for j in range(env.world.world_size):\n      if env._current_state.grid[i, j, workshop_index] == 1:\n        workshop_pos = (i, j)\n        break\n    if workshop_pos is not None:\n      break\n\n  if workshop_pos is None:\n    return []  # Workshop not found\n\n  # Now, generate moves to reach workshop_pos from current_pos.\n  # We'll use the `move` function to generate the sequence of moves.\n  # But note: The `move` function in the DSL is not implemented here.\n  # We'll simulate the move sequence.\n\n  # Calculate the direction to move.\n  dx = workshop_pos[0] - current_pos[0]\n  dy = workshop_pos[1] - current_pos[1]\n\n  # We'll move in the direction of the workshop.\n  # We'll generate a sequence of moves: UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT based on dx, dy.\n  actions = []\n  while dx != 0 or dy != 0:\n    if dx > 0:\n      actions.append(1)  # DOWN\n      dx -= 1\n    elif dx < 0:\n      actions.append(0)  # UP\n      dx += 1\n    if dy > 0:\n      actions.append(3)  # RIGHT\n      dy -= 1\n    elif dy < 0:\n      actions.append(2)  # LEFT\n      dy += 1\n\n  # After moving to the workshop, we need to perform the USE action.\n  actions.append(4)  # USE\n\n  return actions", "island_id": 4, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
