{"timestamp": "2025-09-12T22:29:47.144392", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # TODO: Implement this function using the provided code base.\n  # The function should return a list of action indices that the agent can execute to craft the item.\n\n  # Use the following helper functions:\n  # - env.world.cookbook.recipes: A dict mapping output index to recipe dict.\n  # - env.world.cookbook.index: An Index instance mapping names to integer IDs.\n  # - env.world.cookbook.primitives: A set of primitive item indices.\n  # - env.world.cookbook.environment: A set of environment indices (e.g., boundary, water).\n  # - env.world.cookbook.kinds: A set of all item indices.\n  # - env.world.cookbook.n_kinds: Total number of kinds.\n  # - env.world.grabbable_indices: List of indices for grabbable items.\n  # - env.world.workshop_indices: List of indices for workshop locations.\n  # - env.world.water_index: Index for water.\n  # - env.world.stone_index: Index for stone.\n\n  # You may also use the following methods from CraftState:\n  # - state.next_to(i_kind): Returns True if the agent is next to an item of kind i_kind.\n  # - state.satisfies(goal_name, goal_arg): Returns True if the inventory has the goal item.\n\n  # Use the following helper functions to convert between item names and indices:\n  # - env.world.cookbook.index[item]  # Converts item name to index\n  # - env.world.cookbook.index.get(item)  # Returns index if exists, else None\n\n  # The following actions are available:\n  # - 0: DOWN, 1: UP, 2: LEFT, 3: RIGHT, 4: USE\n\n  # Note: The agent can only craft at the workshop specified in the recipe.\n\n  # Example: To craft an \"AXE\", the agent needs to go to the workshop (workshop0, workshop1, or workshop2)\n  # and then perform a USE action.\n\n  # Important: The function should return a list of actions to craft the item, including moving to the workshop and then using it.\n  # Do not assume the agent is already at the workshop.\n\n  # Implementation details:\n  # 1. Get the index of the item to craft using env.world.cookbook.index[item].\n  # 2. Check if the item is craftable (i.e., it has a recipe in env.world.cookbook.recipes).\n  # 3. If not craftable, return empty list.\n  # 4. Get the required workshop location from the recipe (recipe['_at']).\n  # 5. Convert the workshop name to its index using env.world.cookbook.index[workshop_name].\n  # 6. Check if the agent has all the required items/primatives for crafting.\n  # 7. If not, return empty list.\n  # 8. Move to the workshop (using the direction actions).\n  # 9. Once at the workshop, perform a USE action (4).\n\n  # Additional note: The agent might need to collect some primitives first, but this function should not handle that.\n  # It should assume that the agent has the required items/primatives already (or handle it via the `collect` function).\n\n  # Hints:\n  # - Use env.world.cookbook.recipes to get the recipe for the item.\n  # - Use env.world.cookbook.index to convert workshop name to index.\n  # - Use env.world.workshop_indices to get the list of workshop indices.\n  # - Use env.world.cookbook.index[item] to get the item index.\n  # - The agent needs to be next to the workshop to use it (use state.next_to(workshop_index)).\n\n  # Example of what the code might look like:\n  # workshop_name = recipe['_at']\n  # workshop_index = env.world.cookbook.index[workshop_name]\n  # if workshop_index not in env.world.workshop_indices:\n  #   return []\n  # ... (then move to workshop and use)\n\n  # IMPORTANT: The function should return a list of actions that the agent can take to craft the item.\n  # The actions should be a list of integers (0-4), where 0-3 are movement actions and 4 is USE.\n\n  # Note: This function does not handle collecting primitives. It assumes the agent already has them.\n  # If the agent does not have the required items, it should return an empty list.\n\n  # Let's write the code for craft_v2.\n  pass", "island_id": 0, "scores": {"3": 