{"timestamp": "2025-09-14T00:13:21.233644", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # The following is the implementation of craft_v2. You need to implement it.\n  # We will not have access to the internal state of the environment, we have to use the API of the environment.\n  # We will have to use the following functions:\n  #   - env.step(action) to execute an action and get the new state\n  #   - env.observation() to get the current observation (features, etc.)\n  #   - env.features() to get the current feature vector\n  #   - env.features_dict() to get the current feature dictionary\n  #   - env.satisfies(goal, arg) to check if the goal is satisfied\n  #   - env.next_to(i_kind) to check if the agent is next to an entity of a certain kind\n  #   - env.world.cookbook.recipes to get the recipes (a dictionary)\n  #   - env.world.cookbook.primitives_for(goal) to get the primitives required for crafting the goal\n  #   - env.world.cookbook.index.get(item) to get the index of an item\n  #   - env.world.cookbook.index.get(primitive) to get the index of a primitive\n  #   - env.world.cookbook.index.get(workshop) to get the index of a workshop\n  #   - env.world.cookbook.environment to get the environment set (non-grabbable entities)\n  #   - env.world.grabbable_indices to get the list of grabbable entities\n  #   - env.world.workshop_indices to get the list of workshop indices\n  #   - env.world.water_index to get the index of water\n  #   - env.world.stone_index to get the index of stone\n  #   - env.world.random to get the random number generator\n\n  # We will have to use the following actions:\n  #   - 0: DOWN\n  #   - 1: UP\n  #   - 2: LEFT\n  #   - 3: RIGHT\n  #   - 4: USE\n\n  # The agent can move around the environment using the move actions (0-3) and can use the USE action (4) to craft or collect items.\n  # The agent can only craft at a specific workshop location (one of the workshop_indices).\n  # The agent must first collect all the required primitives (if not already collected) before crafting the item.\n\n  # Steps for craft_v2:\n  # 1. Check if the item is already in the inventory (using env.satisfies(item, item_index)).\n  # 2. If not, get the recipe for the item from env.world.cookbook.recipes.\n  # 3. For each ingredient in the recipe, check if the ingredient is a primitive (in env.world.cookbook.primitives) or an item.\n  # 4. If the ingredient is a primitive, collect it (if not already collected).\n  # 5. If the ingredient is an item, craft it (if not already crafted).\n  # 6. Once all ingredients are collected, go to the workshop location (one of the workshop_indices) specified in the recipe.\n  # 7. Perform the USE action to craft the item.\n\n  # Note: The agent must be next to the workshop location to craft the item.\n\n  # Important: We must use the correct action numbers (0-4) for move and USE actions.\n\n  # We will use the following helper functions:\n  #   - move_to_workshop(workshop_index): moves the agent to the workshop location.\n  #   - collect_primitive(primitive): collects the primitive if not already collected.\n  #   - craft_item(item): crafts the item if not already crafted.\n\n  # However, note that we cannot write helper functions here, we must write the main function.\n\n  # Let's implement the steps:\n\n  # Step 1: Check if the item is already in the inventory.\n  item_index = env.world.cookbook.index.get(item)\n  if env.satisfies(item, item_index):\n      return []  # No actions needed.\n\n  # Step 2: Get the recipe for the item.\n  # The recipe is a dictionary of ingredient: count.\n  recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes.get(item_index)\n  if recipe is None:\n      raise ValueError(f\"Recipe for {item} not found.\")\n\n  # Step 3: For each ingredient in the recipe, check if it's a primitive or an item.\n  # We'll collect all the required primitives and craft all the required items.\n  required_primitives = []\n  required_items = []\n\n  for ingredient, count in recipe.items():\n      # Check if the ingredient is a primitive (in env.world.cookbook.primitives)\n      if ingredient in env.world.cookbook.primitives:\n          required_primitives.append(ingredient)\n      else:\n          required_items.append(ingredient)\n\n  # Step 4: Collect all required primitives.\n  for primitive in required_primitives:\n      primitive_index = env.world.cookbook.index.get(primitive)\n      if not env.satisfies(primitive, primitive_index):\n          # Collect the primitive.\n          # We'll use the collect_primitive function, but we have to write the logic here.\n          # First, move to the primitive location.\n          # We'll use a helper function to move to the primitive location, but we can't write helper functions.\n          # So we'll do it inline.\n          # We need to move to a cell that contains the primitive.\n          # We'll check if the agent is next to the primitive (using next_to).\n          # If not, move towards it.\n          # But note: We can't move towards it without knowing the direction.\n          # Instead, we'll use the following strategy:\n          #   - While not next to the primitive, move randomly until we are next to it.\n          #   - But this is inefficient and may not work.\n          #   - Instead, we'll use the fact that the environment has a feature that shows the primitive.\n          #   - However, we don't have a direct way to get the location of the primitive.\n          #   - We'll have to move until we are next to it.\n\n          # Instead, let's use the following: we'll create a function to move to the primitive.\n          # But we can't, so we'll do it in a loop.\n\n          # This part is tricky. We'll assume that the agent can move until it is next to the primitive.\n          # We'll create a list of actions to move until we are next to the primitive.\n\n          # We'll create a list of actions to move to the primitive.\n          # We'll use a while loop to move until we are next to the primitive.\n          # But note: We cannot run while loops in the function because we are returning a list of actions to take.\n          # We have to return the list of actions to take in one go.\n\n          # Therefore, we cannot do a while loop here. We have to return a sequence of actions that will move the agent to the primitive.\n\n          # However, note that the environment does not provide a way to move to a specific location.\n          # We have to move step by step until we are next to the primitive.\n\n          # But we are not allowed to run while loops in the function because we are returning a list of actions.\n          # We have to return a list of actions that, when executed, will move the agent to the primitive.\n\n          # How can we do that without knowing the location of the primitive?\n          # We cannot. Therefore, we must assume that the agent is already next to the primitive or we have to move randomly until we are.\n\n          # However, note: the environment does not provide a way to get the location of the primitive.\n          # So we have to move randomly until we are next to it.\n\n          # We'll create a list of actions to move randomly until we are next to the primitive.\n          # But note: moving randomly might not be efficient, but it's the only way.\n\n          # We'll create a list of actions to move randomly for a fixed number of steps (e.g., 10 steps).\n          # We'll use the following: move in a fixed pattern (e.g., UP, LEFT, UP, LEFT, ...) until we are next to the primitive.\n\n          # However, we don't know the direction. So we'll use a random direction each time? But we can't use random because we have to return a fixed list.\n\n          # Therefore, we'll have to return a list of actions that moves the agent in a fixed pattern until it is next to the primitive.\n\n          # But note: we are not allowed to use random numbers because the environment might be deterministic.\n\n          # So we'll return a fixed list of actions to move in a fixed pattern (e.g., UP, LEFT, UP, LEFT) for 10 steps.\n\n          # However, this is not guaranteed to work.\n\n          # Given the constraints, we'll assume that the agent is already next to the primitive.\n          # So we'll skip the movement and just use the USE action to collect the primitive.\n\n          # But note: the agent must be next to the primitive to collect it.\n\n          # So if the agent is not next to the primitive, we have to move to it.\n          # But we don't know how.\n\n          # Therefore, we'll return an empty list for now and hope that the agent is next to the primitive.\n          # This is not a good solution, but it's the best we can do without more information.\n\n          # We'll return an empty list for the primitive collection step.\n          # This is a placeholder.\n\n          # We'll return an empty list for the primitive collection.\n          # The agent must have moved to the primitive already.\n\n          # But this is not safe.\n\n          # Alternative: we can check if the agent is next to the primitive using env.next_to(primitive_index).\n          # If not, we'll return a list of actions to move towards the primitive, but we don't know the direction.\n\n          # So we'll return an empty list for now.\n\n          # Note: This is a flaw in the design. We need a better way to move to a location.\n\n          # For the purpose of this exercise, we'll assume that the agent is already next to the primitive.\n\n          # So we'll return an empty list for the primitive collection.\n          pass\n\n  # Step 5: Craft all required items.\n  for item_to_craft in required_items:\n      # Check if the item is already crafted.\n      item_index = env.world.cookbook.index.get(item_to_craft)\n      if not env.satisfies(item_to_craft, item_index):\n          # Craft the item.\n          # We'll need to go to the workshop for the recipe of the item.\n          # The recipe 