{"timestamp": "2025-09-14T00:21:15.230002", "function_name": "craft", "function_body": "  # This is a helper function to get the workshop index for a given item\n  def get_workshop_index(item):\n    # The recipe for the item is stored in env.world.cookbook.recipes\n    # The recipe for the item is a dictionary with a key \"_at\" that specifies the workshop\n    # workshop names in the recipe file are \"workshop0\", \"workshop1\", \"workshop2\"\n    # The index of the workshop in env.world.workshop_indices is the index we need to use\n    # workshop_indices is a list of indices for different types of workshop locations\n    # For example, workshop_indices[0] is the index for workshop0, workshop_indices[1] is for workshop1, etc.\n    # So we need to find which index in workshop_indices corresponds to the workshop name in the recipe\n    # The recipe for the item is stored in env.world.cookbook.recipes[item]\n    # The recipe has a key \"_at\" which is the workshop name\n    # Example: recipe = {\"_at\": \"workshop0\", \"wood\": 2, \"stone\": 1}\n    # We need to find the index of \"workshop0\" in env.world.workshop_indices\n    # But note: workshop_indices might not contain \"workshop0\", it might contain the index for \"workshop0\" as an integer\n    # Actually, workshop_indices is a list of the indices (integers) for the workshops, not the names\n    # So we need to find the index of the workshop name in the index of env.world.cookbook.index\n    # The index of env.world.cookbook.index is a mapping from name to integer index\n    # But the recipe uses the name \"workshop0\", so we need to get the integer index for \"workshop0\" from env.world.cookbook.index\n    # Then we can find the index in env.world.workshop_indices that has that integer index\n    # Actually, env.world.workshop_indices is a list of the integer indices for the workshops, so we can just find the position of the integer index in this list\n    # But note: workshop_indices is a list of the indices (integers) for the workshops, so we can do:\n    # workshop_index = env.world.workshop_indices.index(workshop_name_index)\n    # where workshop_name_index is the integer index of \"workshop0\" from env.world.cookbook.index\n    # However, env.world.cookbook.index is a mapping from name to index, so we can get the index for \"workshop0\" using env.world.cookbook.index[\"workshop0\"]\n    # But note: the recipe uses the name \"workshop0\", but the index might not be the same as the name\n    # So we need to get the integer index for the workshop name from the cookbook index\n    # Let's get the recipe for the item\n    recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes[item]\n    workshop_name = recipe[\"_at\"]\n    # Get the integer index for the workshop name from the cookbook index\n    workshop_index = env.world.cookbook.index[workshop_name]\n    # Now find the position of this integer index in env.world.workshop_indices\n    # workshop_indices is a list of integer indices for the workshops\n    # We need to find the index of workshop_index in workshop_indices\n    workshop_pos = env.world.workshop_indices.index(workshop_index)\n    return workshop_pos\n\n  # This is a helper function to check if we have all the items/ primitves required for crafting\n  def has_all_items(item):\n    # Get the recipe for the item\n    recipe = env.world.cookbook.recipes[item]\n    # We need to check if we have enough of each item in the recipe\n    # The recipe is a dictionary with keys being the item names and values being the required counts\n    # But note: the recipe might not have all the items, and some items might be primitives\n    # We need to check the inventory for each item in the recipe\n    for item_name, count in recipe.items():\n      if item_name == \"_at\":\n        continue  # Skip the workshop key\n      # Get the integer index for the item name from the cookbook index\n      item_index = env.world.cookbook.index[item_name]\n      # Check if we have at least count of this item\n      if env._current_state.inventory[item_index] < count:\n        return False\n    return True\n\n  # Check if we have all the required items for crafting\n  if not has_all_items(item):\n    return []  # Return empty list if we don't have all items\n\n  # Get the workshop index for the item\n  workshop_index = get_workshop_index(item)\n  # Now, we need to go to the workshop and craft the item\n  # We need to find the workshop location in the grid\n  # The workshop locations are in the grid, but we don't know where they are\n  # We need to find a cell that has the workshop kind\n  # The workshop kind is the index we got from workshop_index, but note: workshop_index is the position in workshop_indices, not the kind index\n  # Actually, workshop_index is the index in workshop_indices, which is a list of the kind indices for the workshops\n  # So the kind index for the workshop is workshop_index = env.world.workshop_indices[workshop_pos]\n  # But we already computed workshop_pos, so the kind index is env.world.workshop_indices[workshop_pos]\n  workshop_kind = env.world.workshop_indices[workshop_index]\n  # Now, we need to find the position of a cell that has this workshop_kind\n  # We can search the grid for the first occurrence of workshop_kind\n  # The grid is env._current_state.grid, which is a 3D array of shape (WIDTH, HEIGHT, n_kinds)\n  # We can use np.where to find the position\n  # But note: the grid is a float array, but we can compare to workshop_kind\n  # Find all positions where the grid has workshop_kind in the last dimension\n  # We can do: np.where(env._current_state.grid[..., workshop_kind] > 0)\n  # But note: the grid might have multiple workshops, we just need one\n  # Let's get the position of the first workshop\n  workshop_pos = None\n  for i in range(env._current_state.grid.shape[0]):\n    for j in range(env._current_state.grid.shape[1]):\n      if env._current_state.grid[i, j, workshop_kind] > 0:\n        workshop_pos = (i, j)\n        break\n    if workshop_pos is not None:\n      break\n  if workshop_pos is None:\n    return []  # Workshop not found, return empty list\n\n  # Now, we need to move to the workshop position\n  # Get the current position of the agent\n  current_pos = env._current_state.pos\n  # Compute the direction to move to get to the workshop\n  # We'll move in the direction of the workshop\n  # We can compute the delta in x and y\n  dx = workshop_pos[0] - current_pos[0]\n  dy = workshop_pos[1] - current_pos[1]\n  # Now, we need to move in the direction of dx and dy\n  # We'll move horizontally first, then vertically, or vice versa\n  # But note: the grid is small, so we can move step by step\n  # We'll generate a list of moves to get to the workshop\n  moves = []\n  # Move horizontally\n  if dx > 0:\n    moves.extend([RIGHT] * dx)\n  elif dx < 0:\n    moves.extend([LEFT] * (-dx))\n  # Move vertically\n  if dy > 0:\n    moves.extend([DOWN] * dy)\n  elif dy < 0:\n    moves.extend([UP] * (-dy))\n  # Now, we need to perform the USE action at the workshop\n  # Add the USE action to the moves\n  moves.append(USE)\n  return moves", "island_id": 3, "scores": {"3": -1}}
